  "## What this records

A closing transaction for the session that advanced the clj->kotoba refactor.
It exists so the next agent can resume without the conversation. Read the
`in flight` section first -- six background agents were launched minutes before
this was written and their work is NOT accounted for below.

## The migration work that landed

The task began as `make the kotoba CLI + amu native build actually work`. The
blocker was not that native did not work -- it did -- but that every call
re-verified the artifact, so `nx/execute` cost 1.9-3.6 s. Splitting it into
`prepare` / `invoke` / `close!` in `kototama.native.executor` (tender-native
ab76044, 1915ee1) brought it to 47 ms per call, with `admit-validity!`
re-checking expiry, not-before, and revoked signers per invoke so the split
does not buy speed by dropping a check.

On top of that, a delegation seam was proven across five repos: an
`oracle.cljc` that loads a shipped `*.kir.edn` and executes an export, with no
fallback, plus a test-only generator and two gates (drift, delegation). The
reference implementation is `cloud-itonami-app`'s `kotoba_oracle.cljc`
(98a57c6); calendar, crdt, fsm and app-files reuse the template.

### The boundary, measured rather than assumed

Delegation is bounded by the interpreter's ADT node limit, not by whether a
value is a collection. `mokuroku` refuses 13 catalog items; `fsm` accepts 32
events over an alphabet of 3. The axis is whether the value's SIZE GROWS WITH
THE DOMAIN. An earlier note in this series said `a collection cannot be
delegated`; that was too strong and is corrected here.

Value shapes at `ir/execute`, measured by running the guests: records cross as
`[schema field ...]` in declared order; variants as `[schema case-tag payload]`;
a typed set as `[[:set :keyword] [items...]]` -- NOT a Clojure set; `[:option
:string]` as `[[:option :string] true \"x\"]`; `:document` as `[\"vector\"
[[\"i64\" 50] ...]]`.

### Defects the seams exposed

Writing an oracle forces someone to state what a function means, and that is
where the bugs were:

- `calendar/model.cljc` `overlaps?` accepted zero-duration intervals (fixed,
  d565658)
- `app-files` declared a confinement rule and never enforced it -- `BrowseSource
  -fetch` returned rows outside the granted directory (fixed 4d4a7bd, bound to
  its core by a gate 6572f6c)
- `net-kotobase` treasury: `js/parseInt` returned NaN on an unparseable block
  header, NaN propagated, and a payment that was never confirmed was accepted
  as confirmed -- the Worker returned success where it owed a 402 (fixed
  dfa1143, with a test failing 5 assertions against the old copy and a
  finite-head positive control that passes against both)
- `nexus-x402`: a vendored header named `8a77e516` while the content was
  `848158de` (fixed 6ca13bf, comments only)
- `animeka`: a `:dev` alias `:local/root` named `orgs/kotoba-lang/
  murakumo-generation`, which west never creates -- it resolved only for whoever
  had the pre-rename directory as a leftover checkout (fixed 0c14939)

## The gate discipline worth carrying forward

`app-files`' gate states, per case, the directory each row is IN -- authored,
never computed with the implementation's own `parent-of`. Deriving it would
make the gate a mirror of the implementation: it would agree with ANY
implementation, including a wrong one. Written down, it fails when the real rule
stops recovering from a raw path what the author says the path means. Proven in
three directions (guest always-true: 14 failures; always-false: 12; host
replaced by `starts-with?`: 6, on exactly the descendant and root cases).

## Three fleet detectors, and the errors in them

`scripts/verify-{namespace-collisions,duplicate-registrations,vendored-copies}
.cljs` were built this session. An independent verification pass reproduced all
three and found real defects in two. Both have one shape: a report that reads as
an all-clear when the check did not run.

- `--check --verify-remote` FAILED OPEN. `gh` exits non-zero on an expired token
  and on rate limiting; `github-id` read every failure as nil, so a broken `gh`
  produced `0 CONFIRMED` and exit 0 -- a clean fleet reported in front of 40 real
  duplicate registrations. Now three outcomes: CONFIRMED / REFUTED / UNVERIFIABLE,
  with UNVERIFIABLE failing `--check` on its own. Measured with a `gh` on PATH
  that exits 1: old exits 0, new exits 1.
- `verify-namespace-collisions` walked only `<repo>/src` -- the THIRD instance of
  one error this session. 3,528 -> 3,837 repos (4,828 source trees), 8 -> 11 real
  collisions. The owner sets were wrong where it mattered most: `treasury/
  core.cljc` listed 5 owners with 7 on disk, `pay/x402.cljc` 4 with 6, and both
  missing copies included the one another ADR calls the worst in the fleet.

The narrow-scan error recurred three times in one day, each time in a different
script, twice after being corrected. Prose does not prevent it. What did catch
it was running an independent verification pass over the detectors themselves.

Also corrected: the claim that every duplicate-registration candidate was
confirmed one upstream with no forks. Two candidates are DIFFERENT repositories
sharing a root commit (`org-w3-svg` + `svgraph`; `com-unity-ads` +
`com-unity-api-services`). Retiring either on the offline signal would have
deleted a real project. Both were on disk when that claim was written.

## Duplicate registrations: 35 -> 1

The premise changed under measurement. Resolving all 4,162 west entries through
the GitHub API by repository id (strictly stronger than the on-disk scan) leaves
exactly ONE repository registered at two paths: `kotoba-lang/compiler` +
`kotoba-lang/amu`, id 1297097065. A rename campaign is actively draining the
rest. The other 39 root-commit groups are ORPHAN CHECKOUTS -- directories left
by renames whose west entry is already gone. West will never recreate them.

## In flight -- NOT accounted for above

Six agents were launched immediately before this closing and are still running.
Each may land a merge in a child repo. **A child merge without a west pin
advance leaves the fleet reading a stale tree** -- the exact failure this session
spent the day fixing. The next agent must check for their results and advance
pins.

1. widen `verify-vendored-copies` to all header spellings and non-`src` vendor
   dirs (superproject; at least 7 declared-vendored files are currently invisible)
2. refresh `wasm-webcomponent/src-cljs/vendor/kotoba/kami_host.cljc` -- genuinely
   stale, ~41 diff lines, 2 upstream commits, invisible to the detector on both
   axes
3. investigate the three new `kami/ongaku/e2e/*` collisions across
   kami-ongaku-{notation,sampler,sequencer} (investigation only)
4. ~~check `net-kotobase`'s `pay/core.cljc` + `pay/x402.cljc` for drift~~ --
   **RESOLVED, benign, no change made.** All three vendored copies are
   pin-honest. `pay/core.cljc` and `treasury/core.cljc` are byte-current;
   `pay/x402.cljc` is stale by exactly one strictly-additive commit (`848158d`,
   0 lines removed, +6 symbols, `ns` form byte-identical) that adds a v2 API
   nothing in the repo calls -- `grep` for all six new names returns zero hits.
   Every symbol the gateway actually calls is byte-identical to library HEAD.

   It also fails CLOSED where it matters: a v2-shaped payload has no top-level
   `:scheme`, so the vendored v1 `payload-errors` yields
   `:x402/scheme-mismatch` and `authorize` returns 402. A v2-only buyer is
   turned away with a 402 it is owed and is never served for free -- and that
   coverage gap is identical at library HEAD, so it is a protocol gap for this
   consumer, not drift. Refreshing would add 58 lines of unreachable API and
   correct no behaviour, which is a change that cannot be defended.

   Two corrections to the brief I gave that agent. First, my claim that
   `net-kotobase` has vendored `pay`/`treasury` copies in more than one source
   tree was WRONG -- there is exactly one copy of each, in
   `kotobase-api-gateway-cljs/src/`. The `clj-edge` hits are shadow-cljs
   analysis cache (`.cache.transit.json`), not copies. The `7 owners on disk`
   figure is 7 REPOSITORIES fleet-wide, not 7 copies in this repo; the widened
   collision detector is consistent with that and needs no correction.

   Second, and worth carrying: the header's justification for vendoring at all
   (`CI checks out one repo with no west workspace`) is true only of the
   gateway. The sibling `protocols-worker` reads the LIVE library through
   `../../../kotoba-lang/pay/src` in its `shadow-cljs.edn`, so one repo both
   vendors and does not vendor the same library, for different build units.
5. investigate three self-shadowing namespaces (`kotobase/site_skin.cljc`,
   `etzhayyim/{yoroshiku,bonsai}.cljc`)
6. measure whether the three `:local/root` sites can be repointed from
   `../compiler` to `../amu` (prerequisite for retiring the duplicate entry)

Pin advance, for each child repo they touch:

    nbb scripts/advance-pins.cljs <org> <file-with-repo-name> --execute
    nbb scripts/verify-west-pins.cljs

## Open owner decisions -- recorded, not decided

- **Retire the `compiler` west entry.** Evidence is one-sided: canonical
  full_name is `kotoba-lang/amu`; the `compiler` pin is a strict ancestor 56
  commits behind main while the `amu` pin IS main; every script that names a path
  already names `amu`; a fleet-ci roles gate is already failing on the name
  mismatch. NOT free: three `:local/root` sites (`kotoba/deps.edn`,
  `kototama/deps.edn` x2) resolve to the doomed directory and must be repointed
  in the same change -- see in-flight item 6. The ~104 deps.edn files spelling the
  coordinate `io.github.kotoba-lang/compiler` do NOT block it; GitHub redirects
  them.
- **39 orphan checkouts.** On-disk only, unregistered. Not verified free of
  unpushed commits -- that is `git-cleanup-conflict` territory.
- **`kotoba/lang/package_registry.cljc` divergence.** Bidirectional; 8 of 10
  shared functions are on the acceptance path. Taking core-contracts wholesale
  drops the `definition-cids` check and every definition-pinned package fails
  `:definition/not-locked`; the reverse drops registry-layer checks that
  `verify-lock` re-catches -- the failure MOVES rather than disappears. One
  acceptance-visible difference: an invalid registry with empty requests returns
  `ok? true` on one side.
- **`kami.{fsm,gpu,input}` collisions** between `kami-engine-sdk` and
  `fsm`/`gpu`/`host`.
- **`kami-contracts`: abandoned or authority.** README and behaviour say opposite
  things and nothing in the tree adjudicates. CI red for a month (failing in the
  authority mechanism itself), zero `deps.edn` references, `kami.scene` an
  unmodified pre-split copy still citing an `ARCHITECTURE.md` section that does
  not exist here -- yet the superproject ledger names it authority and a repo in
  another org hand-copied it saying it will follow. Cost is asymmetric: deleting
  it orphans an untested cross-org promise.
- **network-awai's dormant `cert_governor_app` and `media` groups.** The framing
  `wasm dormant, JVM live` was wrong: the whole media subtree publishes nothing.
  `cert_governor` is NOT a port of the production rule -- production gives the
  advisor no disposition channel and the component's containment is LOOSER. All
  three external tools ARE installed as pinned. One wiring line is the blocker.

## Verification state

`verify-west-pins.cljs` passed on every pin advance this session (each reported
fast-forward). `verify-namespace-collisions --check` exits 1 (11 collisions, all
owner decisions). `verify-duplicate-registrations --verify-remote`: 40 CONFIRMED
/ 2 REFUTED / 0 UNVERIFIABLE. Superproject clean apart from pre-existing
untracked `orgs/` directories and a modified `observatory.datoms.edn` that
predate this session. No stashes, no worktrees, no branches of mine remain.

## Resume point

Wait for the six agents, land each result, advance the pin for every child repo
touched, and re-run the three detectors. Beyond that the migration's
decision-free surface is largely exhausted -- what remains is the owner-decision
list above."}]
